    "chunk_size_bytes": 1550,
    "chunk_text": "Sri Lanka has a proud history of applying science to the administration of justice. The Government Analyst\u2019s Department has served the nation for more than a century as the country\u2019s principal forensic science institution. Today, its highly trained forensic scientists provide scientific services to the Sri Lanka Police, the Criminal Investigation Department (CID), the judiciary, and other law enforcement agencies. Through the use of modern technology and internationally accepted scientific methods, they assist in solving thousands of criminal and civil cases each year.\n\nThe work of a forensic scientist extends far beyond laboratory testing. They carefully examine evidence using validated scientific methods, maintain strict quality assurance standards, prepare detailed scientific reports, and present expert testimony before courts. Their responsibility is not to support the prosecution or the defence but to present impartial scientific findings. Their ultimate duty is to assist the court in discovering the truth.\n\nAdvances in scientific technology have significantly strengthened forensic investigations in Sri Lanka. Sophisticated instruments, such as DNA genetic analyzers, real-time PCR systems, gas chromatography-mass spectrometry (GC-MS), high-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC), comparison microscopes, and advanced digital imaging systems, enable scientists to analyse evidence with remarkable sensitivity and accuracy. These technologies allow investigators to detect evidence that may be invisible to the naked eye."
