    "chunk_text": "The Trump administration is putting pressure on the Smithsonian to reshape the truth behind American history, and \u201cto force the museum to align with a more flattering narrative of America\u2019s past\u201d. For example, the administration has altered Donald Trump\u2019s portrait plaque to omit details of his two impeachments. And, according to the wisdom of Florida Governor, Ron DeSantis, slavery was a \u201cjob training program:\u201d\n\nPerhaps the real meaning of July Fourth to the African-American lies in the extracts of perhaps the greatest abolitionist speech, given by Frederick Douglass, on July 4, 1952, before a crowd of white abolitionists in Rochester, New York. A self-educated slave who led the abolitionist movement in the 1850s, Douglass\u2019 words are resonant even to this day:\n\n\u201cFellow citizens, pardon me, allow me to ask, why am I called upon to speak here today? What have I, or those I represent, to do with your national independence? Are the great principles of political freedom and natural justice, embodied in that Declaration of Independence, extended to us? And am I, therefore, called upon to bring our humble offering to the national altar, and to confess the benefits and express devout gratitude for the blessings resulting from your independence to us?\n\n\u201cBut such is not the case. I say it with a sad sense of disparity between us. I am not included within the pale of this glorious anniversary. Your high independence only reveals the immeasurable distance between us. The blessings in which you, this day, rejoice, are not enjoyed in common. The rich inheritance of justice, liberty, prosperity and independence, bequeathed by your fathers, is shared by you, not by me. The sunlight that brought light and healing to you, has brought stripes and death to me. This Fourth July is yours, not mine, You may rejoice, I must mourn."
