    "language": "en",
    "md5": "d92e85bc68436095618fcacf706b248e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1105,
    "chunk_text": "Call to resolve shortage of JPs\n\nThe current system of appointing Justices of the Peace (JP) is open to criticism that \u201canyone can become a JP if he has the right connections.\u201d\u00a0 In contrast, Sworn Translators are appointed through a public and tough examination conducted by the Ministry of Justice. Only those who obtain 50% and above marks, and who have work experience in translations, are appointed. Hence Sworn Translators are a fully qualified and vetted group, mostly senior government servants. I respectfully suggest that the Ministry of Justice appoint JPs from the official Sworn Translator List. Priority could be given to the senior, experienced group, for example those aged 60 years and above.\u00a0 Website notice is enough to attract applicants above 60. At the same time, educated political supporters who meet the educational qualifications could also be considered. This reform will help solve the existing shortage of JPs. In time, all JPs appointed through this method will be highly qualified persons with proven integrity and language skills, which will strengthen public service."
  },
