    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "cb84801e1c307b06efd2e981fca941fc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1636,
    "chunk_text": "The Sahodarayas We No Longer Have\n\nAt a time when the impact of left wing parties was strong, politicians like Merill Fernando of Moratuwa and Henry Peiris of Panadura stood out for their integrity\n\nGeorge Orwell once wrote that in our age, there is no such thing as keeping out of politics, that politics itself is \u201ca mass of lies, evasions, folly, hatred and schizophrenia.\u201d\n\nLooking at Sri Lanka\u2019s present crop of leaders, it is hard to argue with him. None of today\u2019s politicians comes close to the stature of a Senanayake, a Bandaranaike, or an N.M. Perera, a Kadirgamar or a T.B. Jayah. That such quality should have all but vanished from our political top tier seems almost to defy natural law.\n\nIt is against this backdrop that I find myself looking back at three Rathu Sahodarayas I knew between the late 1940s and early 1970s. All three came from modest, middle-class or lower-middle-class backgrounds. All three were men of principle who never once betrayed the people who trusted them. In defiance of Orwell, they practised a politics free of lies, evasions, folly, hatred and schizophrenia. They won their seats without spending a single rupee, built no personal wealth in office, and went home in the end with empty pockets and no pensions.\n\nIn the early 1960s, travelling by CTB double-decker bus from Panadura to an A-Level class in Dehiwala, I watched a lean, fair-skinned gentleman, neatly dressed in a white shirt, red tie, and a white drill coat draped over one arm boarding the bus. A fellow passenger recognised him at once and offered his seat: \u201cWadivenna, Merrile Sahodaraya, ada parlimenthu wageyi.\u201d"
  },
