    "chunk_text": "One day, a storeroom labourer named Bandaiya, distrustful of his own union, sought Mallawaarachchi\u2019s help in drafting a letter about a workplace dispute. Mallawaarachchi typed the letter twice and spent nearly two hours ensuring it was properly prepared. When Bandaiya tried to press a five-rupee note\u2014the equivalent of a full day\u2019s wage\u2014into his hand, Mallawaarachchi flung it away and snapped, \u201cGive me the letter. If you have money, go and hire a lawyer.\u201d\n\nRemembering these three men still brings a smile to my lips and an ache to my chest. They belonged to no dynasty, drew no fortune from public life, and asked for nothing beyond the trust of the people they served. There was no theatre in what they did, no calculation behind the courtesies they showed even to the poorest of their constituents, only a conviction, worn as plainly as Henry Peiris\u2019s bicycle or Mallawaarachchi\u2019s rubber slippers, that public life was meant to serve rather than enrich. Whatever verdict history may render on the LSSP\u2019s politics, it cannot deny the simple integrity of the men who carried its banner through Panadura, Moratuwa and Kollupitiya. They never deceived their supporters with tales of hardship or pretended to have holes in their banyans to win sympathy. The fact that such figures once walked among us\u2014and that we now struggle to name their equals -- reveals something neither flattering nor easily explained about the politics we have accepted since."
