    "chunk_size_bytes": 1817,
    "chunk_text": "It\u2019s a well-rehearsed sequence of events \u2014 and the king, who has been on the throne for less than four years, has already had a good deal of practice. In that time, he\u2019s ushered out Liz Truss, appointed and later bid farewell to Rishi Sunak, and then appointed Mr. Starmer, to whom he will now also bid farewell.\n\nThis audience with the king is known as \u201ckissing hands,\u201d which dates to a time when incoming prime ministers literally kissed the reigning sovereign\u2019s hand to show their loyalty to the crown. These days, they simply shake hands during the short meeting.\n\nOnce he has been appointed by the king, Mr. Burnham will travel to Downing Street, where members of the press will be waiting to capture his entrance to his new home. There is some speculation that Mr. Burnham, in a departure from more recent handover tradition, might forgo the lectern that his predecessors have used to deliver their first message in front of the iconic black door. That remains unclear.\n\nBut we do know a bit about his message, where he will say he is \u201cacutely conscious\u201d of the turnover in leadership in the last decade. Aides said Mr. Burnham would lay out his domestic priorities and promise to give more breathing room to British people struggling with high prices and slow growth.\n\nThe Burnham era will officially start when he walks through the doors of 10 Downing Street. It\u2019s then that the real work of governing begins. He will set about appointing his cabinet of ministers to carry out Labour\u2019s plans as well as to pursue some of his own goals. He\u2019s made pledges to raise living standards, shift power from London to other cities and regions, and set up a branch of the prime minister\u2019s official office in Manchester.\n\nMegan Specia reports on Britain, Ireland and the Ukraine war for The Times."
