    "md5": "ef9e8ec6a3ec2f5d6aef6ef6bf4541bf",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1816,
    "chunk_text": "Universities urged to strengthen community support to prevent dengue outbreaks\n\nColombo, July 20 (Daily Mirror) - Universities cannot combat the growing dengue threat alone and called for greater cooperation from surrounding communities and the health sector to prevent possible outbreaks, Education and Higher Education Deputy Minister Dr. Madura Seneviratne has said.\n\nSpeaking at the launch of the Clinical Management and Standard Guidelines for Surveillance and Prevention of Dengue Disease for all universities in Sri Lanka, held yesterday (20) at the Ministry of Higher Education in Ward Place, Colombo, the Deputy Minister said universities and student hostels, particularly those located in urban areas, face a higher risk of dengue clusters due to population density and environmental conditions.\n\nHe said the newly introduced guidelines would provide universities with a structured approach to identify dengue cases at an early stage, ensure proper clinical management of infected students, strengthen disease surveillance and implement effective measures to prevent mosquito breeding and transmission.\n\nDr. Seneviratne said that similar dengue prevention guidelines had already been introduced for schools and Pirivena educational institutions, and the new university-specific guidelines would further strengthen dengue control measures within the higher education sector.\n\n\u201cDengue prevention is not a responsibility that universities can shoulder alone. Support from the surrounding communities and the health sector is essential to successfully control the spread of the disease,\u201d he said.\n\nThe Deputy Minister added that active participation from students, academic staff and university employees would be vital in implementing dengue prevention programmes and creating safer learning environments."
