    "md5": "0a5706b0a63b9d74a077123ab0b78c94",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1790,
    "chunk_text": "Steep hikes hit offshore firms and professionals in broad corporate registration fee revision\n\nThe government has enacted a substantial upward revision in corporate registration fees, placing a heavier financial burden on the foreign and offshore entities looking to operate within the island.\n\nImplemented through Extraordinary Gazette No. 2496/03, issued by Trade, Commerce, Food Security and Cooperative Development Minister Wasantha Samarasinghe, the new fee structure amends the regulations last updated in October 2022.\n\nWhile the domestic private companies face modest nominal increases, the regulatory adjustments signal a steeper cost of entry for international capital and specialised corporate structures.\n\nThe most aggressive fee escalations are targeted directly at the foreign direct investment vehicles. Under the revised regulations, the cost to register or renew an offshore company has been set at Rs.198,300.\n\nFurthermore, the offshore entities must now deposit a significantly higher initial sum of US $ 115,000 into a local bank account to cover the operational expenses. The overseas companies attempting to register a physical place of business in Sri Lanka will also experience a sharp administrative cost, with the statutory fee now standing at Rs.79,300.\n\nMarket analysts note that these concentrated increases for foreign enterprises could introduce additional friction to the local investment climate, right as the nation attempts to position itself as a competitive commercial hub in South Asia.\n\nDomestically, the gazette also outlines notable increases for corporate structures across the board. The baseline registration for a private limited company has seen a modest rise to Rs.5,200. However, incorporating a public limited company now requires Rs.26,400."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
