    "chunk_size_bytes": 1759,
    "chunk_text": "80% of Israelis say the US respects the freedoms of its people, while just 15% say the same of China. Japan shows another sizable difference (61% vs. 6%).\u00a0 In Bangladesh, Indonesia, Malaysia, Thailand, and the West Bank and East Jerusalem, more say China respects personal freedoms than the US.\n\nPeople in 17 middle-income countries raise more concerns about the United States\u2019 foreign policy than China\u2019s. A median of 75% say that the US interferes in the affairs of other countries a great deal or a fair amount, while 45% say the same of China. In nearly every country surveyed, more people see the US as an interferer than China.\n\nPeople in many of these middle-income countries see China as a more reliable partner than the US,\u00a0 and are more likely to say China contributes to peace and stability around the world, the survey finds.\u00a0 In South Africa, for example, 72% say China is a reliable partner to their country, while 46% say the same of the US. South Africans are much more likely to say that China (64%) accounts for the interests of countries like their own in making international policy decisions than the US (42%).\n\nIn several Latin American countries \u2013 Argentina, Brazil, Colombia and Peru \u2013 similar shares describe the US and China as reliable partners.\n\nIn Pakistan, people are much more likely to say China is a reliable partner than the US (84% vs. 36%). But in the Philippines, many more see the US as a reliable partner than China (81% vs. 42%).\n\nCommenting on the survey, \u201cNewsweek\u201d said that China is no less intent on pursuing its security and economic interests, but it couches its policies as highlighting, what it calls, principles of \u201cmutual respect, peaceful coexistence and mutually beneficial cooperation.\u201d"
