    "md5": "e3cc19e5b3e8a07490f4afcb47b07f3b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1497,
    "chunk_text": "Commenting on the survey, \u201cNewsweek\u201d said that China is no less intent on pursuing its security and economic interests, but it couches its policies as highlighting, what it calls, principles of \u201cmutual respect, peaceful coexistence and mutually beneficial cooperation.\u201d\n\n\u201cThe US is just not contributing to peace and stability and that is why people have less confidence in Donald Trump,\u201d Silver added. Trump\u2019s demands to control Greenland, the American military raid that captured Venezuela\u2019s then-leader Nicol\u00e1s Maduro, the barrage of tariffs fired on Canadian goods last year, Trump\u2019s claim that Canada could be \u201cthe 51st state of the US\u201d\u00a0 and the US handling of the Israeli-Hamas war in Gaza have led to low approval in many countries,\u201d Silver explained.\n\n\u201cBy comparison, we know that China is seen to be a more reliable partner in many places. It\u2019s more likely to be seen to contribute to global peace and stability,\u201d she added.\n\nPeople in the US, where about 6 in 10 held positive views of the US in 2023, now view China and the US similarly. Three years ago, the spread was 32 percentage points in Washington\u2019s favour. But the saving grace is that the US is still ahead of China when it comes to government\u2019s respect for personal freedoms, though the gap is shrinking.\n\nThe Chinese embassy in Washington told \u201cThe Guardian\u201d that the latest poll \u201cdemonstrates that China\u2019s governance achievements and development progress are widely recognised.\u201d"
  },
