    "chunk_size_bytes": 1855,
    "chunk_text": "Health services face uncertainty over circular on acting specialist doctors\n\nColombo, July 21 (Daily Mirror) - A circular issued by the Postgraduate Institute of Medical Sciences (PGIM) has sparked concern over the functioning of specialist medical services, with a doctors' trade union warning that it could disrupt healthcare delivery across the country.\n\nThe circular, issued by the Senior Registrar of the PGIM on June 26 to all Directorates of Studies, states that acting specialist doctors who have completed their specialist medical training but have not yet obtained the PGIM Board Certificate should not independently examine patients, make clinical decisions, or issue medical recommendations within their specialty.\n\nDoctors' Trade Union Alliance for Medical and Civil Rights Chairman and Specialist Dr Chamal Sanjeewa said the directive has created a serious challenge, as many hospitals are already facing a shortage of specialist doctors.\n\nHe said the Health Ministry currently deploys doctors who have completed their local postgraduate specialist training as acting specialists to base hospitals and specialist units across the country to address staffing shortages.\n\nAccording to Dr Sanjeewa, the new instructions make it difficult for these acting specialists to function independently, potentially affecting the delivery of specialist healthcare services.\n\nHe urged the Health Ministry Secretary to immediately issue clear guidelines to all specialist and acting specialist doctors on how the circular should be implemented to avoid disruptions to patient care.\n\nDr Sanjeewa also questioned the timing of the directive, noting that many specialist units in hospitals across the island are currently being managed by acting specialist doctors who have not yet completed their overseas training but continue to provide essential services."
