    "chunk_text": "Iran's paramilitary Revolutionary Guard claimed responsibility for that attack as well as two other attacks on ships on Monday in the waterway. The route around Oman had been the one the US military encouraged ships to use to avoid Iran's control.\n\nTehran also continued strikes on US-allied countries across the Middle East. Jordan's military said Iran targeted it with five drones and three missiles on Tuesday, all of which were intercepted.\n\nAbout 90 per cent of Kuwait's drinking water comes from desalination, making those attacks critical for the country. US strikes have also hit electrical equipment in Iran, while Iranian officials said American attacks had targeted water facilities as well.\n\nThe escalation has pushed up oil prices in recent weeks. Benchmark Brent crude was trading at nearly USD 90 a barrel on Tuesday, while regular petrol in the US rose to an average of USD 4 a gallon, keeping pressure on consumers ahead of the midterm elections this fall.\n\nThe US State Department said in a fresh warning that \"Iran and groups supportive of Iran may target other US interests overseas or at locations associated with the United States and Americans throughout the world.\"\n\nIn summary, Tuesday saw parallel military escalation and diplomatic activity, with Pakistan again entering the picture as a mediator while the US and Iran exchanged more strikes, attacks spread across the region, and disruption in the Strait of Hormuz continued to weigh on shipping and energy prices."
