    "chunk_size_bytes": 1885,
    "chunk_text": "Govt. dismisses false claims over EPF, ETF reforms\n\nLabour Minister and Deputy Finance Minister Dr. Anil Jayantha yesterday dismissed social media claims that the government planned to merge the Employees\u2019 Provident Fund (EPF) and Employees\u2019 Trust Fund (ETF) or transfer their administration to the private sector as \u201cfalse and misleading\u201d.\n\nThere has been growing speculation over the future of the country\u2019s two largest employee retirement savings schemes following the submission of a Cabinet paper on their administration.\n\nJayantha said the Cabinet paper was intended solely to improve the administrative efficiency of the funds and that no decision had been taken to merge the EPF and ETF or alter their ownership and governance.\n\n\u201cThe EPF and ETF, built through the hard-earned savings of employees, are secure. The government has not taken any decision to merge these funds or hand over their administration to the private sector,\u201d he said.\n\nAccording to the minister, the proposal seeks to appoint a committee of officials to recommend ways to streamline administration, improve the integration of data systems, strengthen investment management and enhance benefits available to members.\n\nAt present, the EPF\u2019s administrative functions are handled by the Department of Labour while the fund itself is managed by the Central Bank of Sri Lanka. The ETF operates under a separate institutional framework.\n\nJayantha said maintaining multiple administrative structures had created operational inefficiencies, with workers often facing delays in accessing their savings due to discrepancies such as minor differences in names across databases.\n\nHe added that billions of rupees remained in unidentified or unresolved accounts because of poor integration between existing data systems, highlighting the need for a unified digital platform to improve service delivery."
  },
