    "md5": "1a3f24e4ad7ea7956eb524eb97890485",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1550,
    "chunk_text": "Despite \u2018Clean Sri Lanka\u2019, our cities are becoming dirtier and messier\n\nPiles of garbage can be seen in many places despite clean up campaigns\n\nSouth Asia suffers from an ungainly reputation for being dirty and messy. That is not always an exaggeration, for the region is overpopulated; its explosive population growth in the past has burdened the states and thinned out per-capita social investment; and local government bodies have often done a sloppy job of keeping their precincts clean and organised.\n\nSri Lanka, by and large, has been an outlier to this particular South Asian ailment \u2014 though the locals have to be reminded of that by visiting Indian tourists \u2014 who, with genuine conviction or clickbait shenanigans, post rave social media reviews about how clean this country is.\u00a0 Such compliments ought to be taken with a pinch of salt, for they are often subjective assessments against the innate filth of most South Asian cities, and not global comparisons.\n\nHowever, Sri Lanka risks losing even these small luxuries. It is a sad indictment for the government\u2019s much hyped Clean Sri Lanka campaign that the garbage keeps piling up along the city streets uncollected for days, basic public amenities such as walking paths and pavements are in disrepair and encroached by the hawkers and shopfronts, and public toilets are as dirty as they were before. In the meantime, well-meaning past programmes such as the relocation of slum dwellers into apartments elsewhere have not only stopped; the process itself seems to have reversed."
  },
