    "md5": "7ea591753f61e8fb6f0f70cd69b545f3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1673,
    "chunk_text": "However, Sri Lanka risks losing even these small luxuries. It is a sad indictment for the government\u2019s much hyped Clean Sri Lanka campaign that the garbage keeps piling up along the city streets uncollected for days, basic public amenities such as walking paths and pavements are in disrepair and encroached by the hawkers and shopfronts, and public toilets are as dirty as they were before. In the meantime, well-meaning past programmes such as the relocation of slum dwellers into apartments elsewhere have not only stopped; the process itself seems to have reversed.\n\nWorse still, public institutions, elected or otherwise, are among the main contributors to pollution. Municipalities and urban councils leave their garbage trucks unattended by the roadside or pile garbage on prime state land, creating health hazards to everyone living around. State-of-the-art waste disposal and recycling is not explored not due to the lack of investor interest, but because it would cut off wheeler-dealers long entrenched in the business.\n\nThe Railway Department piles up its discarded concrete sleepers along the rail track, causing a public safety hazard and an eyesore.\u00a0 State bus depots are a prime breeding ground for mosquitoes, as recent checks by public health inspectors have revealed.\n\nClean Sri Lanka has so far failed to produce tangible results, except for news headlines. Sri Lankan cities, towns, and hamlets are dirtier than they were before. Some commonsense initiatives such as relocating hawkers in the city of Kandy are welcomed, but by and large, inefficient local governments and the central government have become the problem, rather than problem solvers."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-22-dailymirrorlk-fbc52bc2",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-fbc52bc2",
    "date_str": "2026-07-22",
    "description": "Despite \u2018Clean Sri Lanka\u2019, our cities are becoming dirtier and messier",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Despite-Clean-Sri-Lanka-our-cities-are-becoming-dirtier-and-messier/172-346265",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1784661362.0,
