    "language": "en",
    "md5": "bb02b69db1528c0b0534aee26ae70382",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1937,
    "chunk_text": "Third, incompetence of elected representatives is magnified by an incompetent bureaucracy at the local government level. That\u00a0 is a major shortfall in Sri Lankan administrative structure, which many advocates of devolution have often overlooked.\n\nIt takes time for these everyday maladies to show up with their cumulative effect. As Adam Smith once said, it takes a great deal for the ruin of a nation. Nonetheless, the rot sets in, and over time decay eats into the core.\n\nPerhaps, there are a few easy fixes the government could resort to.\n\nFirst, the President can set the basic benchmarks for local government bodies under his Clean Sri Lanka program, one of which should be the provision of basic public services. That is also the model akin to performance-based legitimacy and evaluation, many of its ideological bedfellows, such as the Chinese Communist Party, have resorted to in local governance.\n\nSecondly, it must come to terms with the fact that though most of its mayors, chairmen/ women and elected representatives may be idealistic, they are not equipped for the task they were elected for. Study visits to modern cities in China, East Asia, or Singapore would shed light on how modern cities are governed, which they could emulate back home.\n\nThirdly, the government should build a competent bureaucracy at the local-government level, which similarly requires foreign training and exposure to the best practices of successful states and city governments.\n\nFourthly, there is a radical idea, which Sri Lanka can experiment with someday in the future. The voters should be offered a competitive alternative to elected bodies.\u00a0 American-style Freedom cities, tech-driven new cities with private sector administration often set up as incubation hubs for growth, would be an idea worth exploring. Perhaps Hambantota, sparsely populated with large state-owned land, could well be the laboratory for the first such experiment."
  },
