    "md5": "1c6b3668c470f93fa1e42218f6e0d59a",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1775,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cSri Lanka has every ingredient other markets have built on \u2014 high literacy, English-medium capability, a young population, and no shortage of ambition,\u201d said Teen Academy and Business Athletes Ltd. Founder Bradley Emerson. \u201cOur schools already give students a strong academic foundation. What Teen Academy adds is the capability to apply it \u2014 to build something real, pitch it, and learn what it takes to lead, well before they turn eighteen.\u201d\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s literacy rate stands at roughly 92%, among the highest in South Asia, with near-universal secondary enrolment. But each year, more than 450,000 students sit the G.C.E. Ordinary Level examination, and only around 40,000 go on to secure a place in a state university. Teen Academy\u2019s founder sees an opportunity to give students \u2014 regardless of which path they take next \u2014 practical experience building, pitching, and leading a real venture, as a complement to the strong academic grounding Sri Lankan schools already provide.\n\nIn the Colombo District alone, an estimated 21,000 to 30,000 students aged 13 to 17 attend private and international schools charging annual fees ranging between\u00a0 Rs. 1 m to over Rs. 1.5 m \u2014 families already investing heavily in an educational edge, but without access to a structured, internationally benchmarked business credential for their children.\n\nTeen Academy\u2019s model draws on two established international benchmarks. In the United States, organisations such as Junior Achievement (founded in 1919) and university-run programs at Wharton, Harvard, Babson, Berkeley, Georgetown and NYU have shown that teenagers who build and pitch real ventures arrive at university and the workforce demonstrably better prepared than peers who only study business in theory."
  },
