    "md5": "c062e1c96716f0c289f633eebaf25064",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1872,
    "chunk_text": "Police urge vehicle rental firms to tighten security amid rising fraud\n\nColombo, July 22 (Daily Mirror) - Police have urged vehicle rental companies to strengthen their security measures, warning that fraudsters are using fake identities, suspicious payment methods and deceptive tactics to obtain rental vehicles before disappearing without a trace.\n\nPolice said that rental operators should not hand over vehicles without thoroughly verifying the identity of customers, noting that a few extra minutes spent on verification could prevent the loss of a vehicle worth millions of rupees.\n\nAccording to Police, rental companies should inspect original National Identity Cards (NICs) and driving licences, ensure the person collecting the vehicle matches the identification documents, and obtain a recent photograph or selfie for their records. They also recommend verifying the customer's permanent address through a recent utility bill or Grama Niladhari certificate and confirming that the provided mobile phone number is active through a live call or one-time password (OTP).\n\nPolice have also advised operators to strengthen technical security by installing reliable GPS tracking devices in every rental vehicle and ensuring they remain operational at all times. CCTV cameras should also be installed at vehicle collection and drop-off points to capture clear footage of customers.\n\nIn a bid to make suspects easier to trace, Police recommend avoiding cash transactions and accepting payments only through credit cards, debit cards or bank transfers linked to a Sri Lankan bank account in the customer's name.\n\nPolice further advised rental businesses to sign legally binding rental agreements, obtain verified guarantors for long-term rentals and clearly state that the vehicle cannot be driven by anyone other than the registered renter or subleased to a third party."
  },
