    "md5": "b8d4c83060c9c247eba1ab5fb2e6dd81",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1947,
    "chunk_text": "Kapur's visit followed an earlier inspection of the project in May by Nicole Chulick, Principal Deputy Assistant Secretary in the Bureau of South and Central Asian Affairs at the US State Department. During her visit, Chulick met senior officials of Maldives Ports Limited (MPL), toured the existing Male' Commercial Port and visited the Thilafushi development site. She also expressed US interest in supporting infrastructure development projects across the Maldives.\n\nUnder the Muizzu administration's plan to relocate the country's commercial port, Maldives Ports Limited awarded the design and survey work for the first phase of the project to China Harbour Engineering Company (CHEC) in February. The first phase, known as the Local Harbor Relief Project, includes construction of a 650-metre quay wall, a 125-metre jetty, X-ray screening facilities, automated cargo handling systems and a bonded warehouse.\n\nThe involvement of both China and the United States in connection with the Thilafushi project illustrates the increasingly competitive environment surrounding maritime infrastructure across the Indian Ocean. While Beijing continues to play a major role through engineering and construction projects, Washington is seeking to expand its engagement through diplomatic outreach, investment partnerships and supply-chain cooperation.\n\nFor the Maldives, the priority remains transforming Thilafushi into a modern commercial port capable of supporting economic diversification and long-term growth. However, as major powers deepen their interest in strategically located infrastructure, the country's ports are becoming more than commercial assets. Like Colombo and other Indian Ocean hubs, Thilafushi is increasingly emerging as a focal point where economic development, maritime security and geopolitical competition converge, underscoring the challenge facing smaller states seeking investment while preserving their strategic autonomy."
