    "chunk_text": "Some studies show that mosquitoes are attracted to certain colours worn by individuals \u2013 red, orange, and black being most preferred. Individual body odour has been attributed as an attractant for these mosquitoes, and some point out that the individual genetic constitution is also an important factor. A study published in 2015 found that DNA may account for nearly 67% of mosquito attraction. Involving sets of twins,\n\nThe factors that make an individual more attractive to mosquitoes may be at work when we notice that only some in a group are harassed by them, while the others are spared most of the time.\n\nJudging by the pictures we see every day in visual media makes one wonder whether we could ever eradicate Aedes mosquitoes\u2019 breeding sites. The top Aedes mosquito breeding sites have been factory and construction sites, government buildings, prisons, religious places, schools and hospitals\u2026 Cleaning programmes should be carried out by each establishment with daily drills supervised by a sanitary officer.\n\nElimination of breeding sites does not imply the complete elimination of the mosquito from the island but reducing the prevalence of Aedes to an extent that can no longer be a threat."
  },
