    "chunk_size_bytes": 2007,
    "chunk_text": "Judging by the pictures we see every day in visual media makes one wonder whether we could ever eradicate Aedes mosquitoes\u2019 breeding sites. The top Aedes mosquito breeding sites have been factory and construction sites, government buildings, prisons, religious places, schools and hospitals\u2026 Cleaning programmes should be carried out by each establishment with daily drills supervised by a sanitary officer.\n\nElimination of breeding sites does not imply the complete elimination of the mosquito from the island but reducing the prevalence of Aedes to an extent that can no longer be a threat.\n\nThere are problems with other methods of control as well. Some dengue-infected people may remain asymptomatic, and those with milder infections may not seek medical intervention as required. This fact leaves a considerable reservoir of the virus in the community for the feeding Aedes. The rush seems to be more oriented to catching and persecuting people who harbour breeding sites rather than educating the public, particularly the teachers, students, parents and even the health workers involved in the control programme. I assume that I have presented a fair description of the behaviour of the Aedes mosquitoes and a few of the control measures used by our Ministry of Health to combat the spreading scourge.\n\nViral epidemics have a fluctuating pattern, peaking and then declining on their own. Remember the Covid-19 pandemic. However, the alarming spread of dengue must be tackled immediately. The launching of the \u201cStop Dengue Now\u201d programme is timely and commendable. This programme was officially launched a few days back. The programme is to be implemented by the Ministry of Health, mass media, and the private sector and media partners to support the national response to the ongoing dengue epidemic.\n\nLeave a Reply Cancel replyYour email address will not be published. Required fields are marked *Comment * Name *\n\nSave my name, email, and website in this browser for the next time I comment."
