    "md5": "0486718d8d34edf4a1e552946a842ffa",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1830,
    "chunk_text": "El Ni\u00f1o and cloud seeding\n\nRCyAF/ SLAF, Air Ceylon, Air Lanka, Singapore Airlines and SriLankan Airlines\n\nMeteorologists have declared that the weather phenomenon known as \u2018El Ni\u00f1o\u2019 is upon us. Monsoon rains are anticipated to fail, and farmers will face untold hardship. No water will be available for hydroelectric power, and if the forecasts are correct and the situation continues, soon there will be regular power supply interruptions. In short, the country will be facing a potential crisis.\n\nWhich leads me to wonder whether our officials have considered the practice of seeding the clouds. The process called \u2018cloud seeding\u2019 can enhance potential rain clouds to produce rain and mitigate the effects of \u2018El Ni\u00f1o\u2019, thus improving the water table. Raindrops usually form around small particles, like dust, ice and salt crystals, called nuclei. By introducing more nuclei into a cloud for the raindrops to form around, the rain cloud could be enhanced. The chemicals used for nuclei production are silver iodide (AgI) crystals and dry ice (carbon dioxide). The \u2018seeds\u2019 are dispersed into the cloud by using airplanes equipped with electronically activated flares, ground-based rockets, anti-aircraft shells from the ground, or by creating large fires to generate smoke which would provide sufficient nuclei.\n\n(Some readers may remember that many years ago, when the Kolonnawa oil tanks burned, after an LTTE attack, the smoke cloud rose to a high altitude and then mushroomed out, producing rain.)\n\nThe last of these options is possible only when there is no wind. In our case, however, the Southwest monsoon winds have already set in. These masses of air have travelled literally thousands of miles over the sea and are laden with moisture.\n\nThe Trailing Edge of the wing with Silver Iodide Flares working"
  },
  {
