    "chunk_size_bytes": 1374,
    "chunk_text": "The easiest way to seed clouds would be from the air. The Sri Lanka Air Force has aircraft and experienced pilots who can carry out such an operation effectively. If we seed the clouds to the south of Ratmalana, rain will fall somewhere northeast of that point. This will, of course, be predicated by the prevalent wind direction and speed for that day.\n\nI believe that the Sri Lankan Air Force has the right sort of airplane for the job. If not, we could use readings from airliners departing from Katunayake, on the Colombo-Singapore route, to estimate the actual wind direction and speed. The target area of rainfall should be over Kotmale, which has the most critical reservoir during the Southwest Monsoon, and fortuitously that route passes overhead Kotmale.\n\nWe could also ask China, India and Thailand for expert advice. There are so many possibilities if we only care to look. It can be done. The benefit of rain at the correct places at this point of time outweighs the cost of such an operation.\n\nAs the old adage goes, desperate times need desperate measures. So, why not give it a try?\n\nBehavioural patterns of mosquitoes and their impact on spread of Dengue infection\n\nLeave a Reply Cancel replyYour email address will not be published. Required fields are marked *Comment * Name *\n\nSave my name, email, and website in this browser for the next time I comment."
