    "num": "dailymirrorlk-50dd02bc",
    "date_str": "2026-07-23",
    "description": "Black July  that gave birth to two  political  bloodbaths",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/Black-July-that-gave-birth-to-two-political-bloodbaths/172-346380",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1784748168.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-23-dailymirrorlk-50dd02bc-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "45e65cf6af47e4569c885307a04ae710",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1816,
    "chunk_text": "Black July  that gave birth to two  political  bloodbaths\n\nBusiness establishments, factories and movie theatres run by Tamils were torched, sometimes while the occupants were in them (Courtesy The Guardian UK)\n\nThe hate instilled in the minds of Tamil youth by the week-long anti Tamil pogrom provided thousands of recruits for the Tamil armed groups\n\nExactly 43 years ago, on July 23 1983,\u00a0 a single violent incident in Jaffna shifted the course of Sri Lankan history by a wide angle. It accelerated an armed conflict in the north of the country that was then marked by random, isolated incidents into a full-blown war which became one of the longest armed conflicts in the world.\n\nIt also triggered a fresh armed conflict in the south which\u00a0 within two years\u00a0 claimed over 60,000 lives, mainly innocent people. The brutality of the armed forces and\u00a0 vigilante groups created by the same forces during that conflict could be assessed by comparing its death toll with that of the former which ran into just over a hundred thousand within three decades.\n\nBoth bloodbaths could have been averted had those power-hungry politicians in power then acted prudently and farsightedly. In fact, it was the government that created the southern conflict \u2013 one between the armed forces assisted by\u00a0 vigilante groups,\u00a0 and the Janatha Vimukthi Peramuna (JVP) which had by then renounced violence after their failed insurrection in 1971 to enter into democratic politics.\n\nThirteen soldiers were killed in Jaffna on July 23, 1983 in a land mine blast followed by gunfire by the Liberation Tigers of Tamil Eelam (LTTE), the most ruthless\u00a0 among half a dozen Tamil armed groups then active in the Northern and Eastern Provinces who were fighting to carve out a separate state for Tamils, within the territory of Sri Lanka."
  },
