    "md5": "4a5b86adae3a3052412a44fc05ce9e12",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1972,
    "chunk_text": "New Animal Welfare Bill: Rs. 250,000 fine, two-year jail for animal cruelty\n\nColombo, July 23 (Daily Mirror) - Those convicted of animal cruelty could face fines of up to Rs. 250,000, imprisonment for up to two years, or both, under Sri Lanka's long-awaited Animal Welfare Bill, which has now been published in the Government Gazette.\n\nThe proposed legislation, which is expected to be presented to Parliament, seeks to replace the century-old Prevention of Cruelty to Animals Ordinance (Chapter 475) with a tougher legal framework aimed at preventing cruelty and improving animal welfare across the country.\n\nUnder the Bill, it will be an offence to beat, kick, torture, overload, overbreed, abandon or poison animals, or intentionally deprive them of food, water or shelter. Keeping animals in cages or enclosures that do not provide sufficient space for movement will also be punishable.\n\nThe Bill imposes even harsher penalties for serious offences. Anyone found guilty of killing an animal in a cruel manner or intentionally killing or selling a pregnant animal could face fines of up to Rs. 500,000 and prison terms ranging from three to four years.\n\nIt also prohibits setting traps to injure or kill animals without reasonable cause, tying or restraining birds in a cruel manner, performing medical or surgical procedures on animals without proper qualifications, and using animals for cosmetic testing except in prescribed circumstances.\n\nThe proposed law further cracks down on organised animal abuse by banning sport hunting, shooting matches, animal fighting and baiting. Property owners who knowingly allow such activities on their premises could also face legal action.\n\nThe commercial sale of animals will also come under tighter regulation. Pet shops and breeding centres will be required to obtain licences from local authorities, while the sale of animals on streets or public places and the sale of pets to persons under the age of 16 will be prohibited."
  },
