    "md5": "137a7387f408679ce931dc6398482afd",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1168,
    "chunk_text": "The commercial sale of animals will also come under tighter regulation. Pet shops and breeding centres will be required to obtain licences from local authorities, while the sale of animals on streets or public places and the sale of pets to persons under the age of 16 will be prohibited.\n\nTo strengthen enforcement, all offences under the proposed law will be cognizable, allowing police to make arrests without a warrant. Designated Animal Welfare Officers will also be empowered to issue directions requiring owners to provide food, shelter or veterinary treatment, while courts will have the authority to confiscate animals and equipment used in cruelty offences upon conviction.\n\nThe legislation also provides for the establishment of an Animal Welfare Advisory Committee comprising representatives from government institutions, law enforcement, academia, animal welfare organisations and the livestock sector to oversee the implementation of the law.\n\nIf enacted by Parliament, the Animal Welfare Bill will mark Sri Lanka's most significant overhaul of animal protection laws, introducing stronger safeguards against cruelty and stricter penalties for offenders."
