    "chunk_size_bytes": 1986,
    "chunk_text": "Houthis claim attack on oil tankers as US launches more strikes on Iran\n\nBBC - Yemen's Houthis say they have attacked two Saudi oil tankers in the Red Sea as the US launched its 12th consecutive night of strikes on Iran.\n\nIranian state media said two people had been killed in the US strikes on Wednesday, and a tanker caught fire in an explosion in a mined route in the Strait of Hormuz.\n\nAhead of the latest round of strikes, US President Donald Trump had threatened to destroy an Iranian bridge or power plant if Iran attacked a ship in the strait and to \"take care of\" the Houthis if they blocked the Red Sea.\n\nIf confirmed, the Houthi attack would be the first since the Iran-backed group announced a \"maritime embargo\" against Saudi Arabia.\n\nThe Houthis named the tankers as the Encelia and the Layla, though their responsibility has not been confirmed.\n\nThe group's military spokesperson Yahya Saree said they had targeted the ships with drones and missiles for allegedly violating its naval blockade.\n\nSaree added that the Houthis promised to continue their naval operations and would continue \"enforcing the 'siege for a siege' equation\".\n\nThe state-run Saudi Press Agency confirmed early on Thursday that the Encelia was struck \"while sailing in the Red Sea\", resulting in a fire on its bow.\n\nCiting an unnamed official source at the Transport General Authority, it described the attack as a violation of international law. All crew were safe, it reported.\n\nUK Maritime Trade Operation (UKMTO) reported an \"unknown projectile\" struck a Saudi tanker about 130km from Al Shuqaiq in Saudi Arabia at 20:00 GMT. The attack caused a fire but no casualties, it said.\n\nBritish maritime risk management group Vanguard also reported that the Saudi tanker Encelia had been hit.\n\nUS Central Command (Centcom) said at 21:30 GMT it had launched strikes at Trump's direction \"to further degrade Iran's ability to threaten civilian mariners and commercial vessels transiting regional waters\"."
