    "chunk_text": "US Central Command (Centcom) said at 21:30 GMT it had launched strikes at Trump's direction \"to further degrade Iran's ability to threaten civilian mariners and commercial vessels transiting regional waters\".\n\nKuwait, Jordan and Bahrain soon reported responding to airborne threats, before Iran's military said they had targeted US assets in all three countries, according to AFP.\n\nIranian state media reported two people had been killed in US strikes on the country's south near its border with Iraq.\n\nMeanwhile, the Iranian Revolutionary Guard (IRGC) said the Strait of Hormuz was \"completely closed\" after an explosion in the channel, Iranian state media reported.\n\nThe IRGC said it had stopped three oil tankers from passing through the strait.\n\nOne tanker caught fire and two others turned back after attempting to pass through a mined route south of the strait, according to Iranian state media.\n\nIt comes after Trump promised earlier on Wednesday to attack an Iranian bridge or power plant if its forces hit ships in the Strait of Hormuz.\n\nIran's Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi later warned of an \"eye for an eye\" response to attacks on Iran.\n\nOn Tuesday, Trump told reporters he would \"take care of the Houthis\" should they obstruct the Red Sea as they had said.\n\nFew details of the \"maritime embargo\" were given by the Houthis when it was announced on Monday, but a media official said they were closing the Bab al-Mandab Strait - the Red Sea's southern gateway - to Saudi vessels.\n\nThe Red Sea has become crucial for oil shipments from Saudi Arabia during the US-Israeli war with Iran, which has led to the effective closure of the Strait of Hormuz in the Gulf.\n\nNow, the threat from the Houthis risks further disruption to international shipping, with hostilities in the region having already led to global fuel prices fluctuating significantly."
