    "chunk_size_bytes": 1845,
    "chunk_text": "Research consistently shows that diverse leadership teams outperform less diverse organisations. Boards and executive management benefit from broader perspectives when evaluating risks, identifying opportunities, and making strategic decisions. Diversity reduces groupthink while encouraging more balanced discussions.\n\nThe younger workforce also brings new expectations. Millennials and Generation Z increasingly seek employers that prioritise inclusion, flexibility, wellbeing, sustainability, and social responsibility. Organisations with strong ESG commitments are becoming more attractive employers in highly competitive labour markets.\n\nEmployee wellbeing has expanded beyond traditional occupational safety. Modern organisations invest in mental health programmes, flexible work arrangements, continuous learning, career development, and work-life balance initiatives. These investments improve productivity while reducing turnover and absenteeism.\n\nInclusive recruitment practices help organisations access wider talent pools. Removing unconscious bias from recruitment, providing equal career opportunities, supporting persons with disabilities, and promoting fair remuneration strengthen both social responsibility and organisational performance.\n\nTraining and development remain essential for creating inclusive workplaces. Diversity awareness programmes, leadership development, anti-harassment policies, and inclusive management practices help build respectful organisational cultures where all employees can succeed.\n\nSupply chain responsibility has also become part of the social pillar. Businesses are expected to ensure ethical labour practices, fair wages, safe working conditions, and respect for human rights throughout their supplier networks. International buyers increasingly conduct ESG audits before selecting suppliers."
  },
