    "md5": "937145acb2dfe601f30ba6e313efbc18",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1565,
    "chunk_text": "With an Exclusive Economic Zone (EEZ) that is several times larger than its land area, Sri Lanka possesses vast untapped marine resources. These include fisheries, shipping, ports, renewable energy, marine biotechnology, aquaculture, coastal tourism, and ocean-based research. Harnessing these opportunities responsibly could contribute significantly to economic recovery, export earnings, employment generation, and climate resilience.\n\nSustainable fisheries remain another critical component of the Blue Economy. The fisheries sector supports thousands of coastal families and contributes significantly to food security and exports. However, overfishing, illegal fishing practices, marine pollution, and climate change threaten the long-term sustainability of fish stocks. Responsible fisheries management, science-based catch limits, modern monitoring systems, and community participation are essential to ensuring healthy marine ecosystems while protecting livelihoods for future generations.\n\nAquaculture also presents enormous growth potential. Sustainable fish farming, shrimp cultivation, seaweed production, and shellfish farming can reduce pressure on wild fish populations while creating new export opportunities. Advances in environmentally friendly aquaculture technologies allow producers to increase yields while minimising impacts on coastal ecosystems. As global demand for sustainably sourced seafood continues to rise, Sri Lanka can strengthen its position in premium international markets by maintaining high environmental and quality standards."
  },
