    "chunk_id": "2026-07-23-dailyftlk-422942ba-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "509576f9b6dbcc6a24b5dd6ee9006611",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1770,
    "chunk_text": "For a commercial port, that question also extends to the environment in which we operate. Every port interacts with its surrounding marine environment. The responsibility is not simply to minimise impacts, but to better understand them so that future decisions are informed by sound science rather than assumption.\n\nHIP provides researchers with access to a unique operational setting where long-term scientific observation can take place alongside commercial port activities. The University contributes scientific expertise, research discipline and independent analysis. Together, the partnership demonstrates another important role businesses can play: creating opportunities for knowledge.\n\nThis month, Dr. M.F.M. Fairooz will present research arising from this work at the 16th International Coral Reef Symposium in Auckland, bringing Sri Lankan scientific research to one of the world\u2019s leading forums on coral reef science.\n\nFor us, the significance lies not only in the findings themselves but in the principle behind them. Scientific evidence helps businesses make better decisions. It strengthens environmental management and enables more informed planning as ports continue to develop. Ultimately, that is how we view ESG. It is not a programme that sits alongside the business. It is part of how the business operates.\n\nThe future of ESG will not be measured by how many projects a company completes or how much it spends. More and more, It will be measured by how effectively businesses integrate environmental responsibility, social value and good governance into the way they grow, operate and create long-term value.\n\nThat is the journey HIPG has been on for the past eight years, and one we believe will define the next generation of sustainable ports."
  },
