    "md5": "0fee784e2176fbac60e2715104f99c3b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1349,
    "chunk_text": "Ethical Supply Chains: What Consumers Expect Today\n\nNot long ago, a company\u2019s supply chain was something almost nobody outside the business ever thought about. Customers cared about price, quality, and maybe the brand\u2019s reputation, but rarely asked where the raw materials came from or who assembled the final product. That has changed, and it\u2019s changed faster than many businesses were prepared for. A string of factory disasters, investigative reports on forced labour, and simple access to information through social media have made supply chains a subject customers actually pay attention to, and increasingly, a subject they judge companies on.\n\nClothing has been at the centre of this shift for years, partly because of how visible the industry\u2019s problems have been. Reports of unsafe factory conditions and workers paid far below a living wage aren\u2019t new, but the willingness of customers to act on that information is. A growing share of shoppers, especially younger ones, say they check where a garment was made before buying it, and some brands have built their entire identity around transparency, publishing the names and locations of every factory they use. Whether every customer actually reads that information is debatable, but the fact that brands feel compelled to publish it at all shows how much the pressure has grown."
  },
