    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "734aa1b606ac60fce2fce3b47904dd54",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1953,
    "chunk_text": "The Future of Ports Will Be Measured Beyond Throughput\n\nCICT became the first terminal in the Port of Colombo to fully electrify its RTG fleet\n\nCICT\u2019s dedicated charging station keeps the terminal\u2019s electric ITVs powered, advancing its commitment to sustainable operations\n\nFor decades, ports have been judged by the numbers that define operational success. Throughput, crane productivity, vessel turnaround time and berth utilisation have long been regarded as the key indicators of performance. They remain fundamental to global trade.\n\nToday, however, another set of metrics is becoming just as important.\n\nHow much energy does a terminal consume? How much carbon has it eliminated? How efficiently does it use water and other resources? How does it coexist with the communities and ecosystems that surround it?\n\nAround the world, the definition of a successful port is changing. Increasingly, ports are being judged not only by how efficiently they move cargo, but by how responsibly they do it.\n\nThis reflects a broader shift in Environmental, Social and Governance (ESG) thinking. For many years, sustainability in the corporate world was often associated with Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR), where environmental and community initiatives existed alongside the business. Today, leading port operators are embedding sustainability into the way terminals are designed, powered and operated.\n\nRather than treating ESG as a separate programme, it is becoming part of operational strategy. Few examples illustrate this transition more clearly than Colombo International Container Terminals (CICT).\n\nAs one of Sri Lanka\u2019s largest deep-water container terminals and a member of the China Merchants Port network, CICT has steadily integrated environmental performance into its day-to-day operations. The terminal\u2019s electrification programme demonstrates how environmental responsibility and operational efficiency can reinforce one another."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-23-dailyftlk-bd113fa1",
    "num": "dailyftlk-bd113fa1",
    "date_str": "2026-07-23",
    "description": "The Future of Ports Will Be Measured Beyond Throughput",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.ft.lk/business/The-Future-of-Ports-Will-Be-Measured-Beyond-Throughput/34-795042",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailyftlk",
    "time_ut": 1784797371.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-23-dailyftlk-bd113fa1-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "e2afce141446c0ddbf07f288a888538f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1983,
    "chunk_text": "As one of Sri Lanka\u2019s largest deep-water container terminals and a member of the China Merchants Port network, CICT has steadily integrated environmental performance into its day-to-day operations. The terminal\u2019s electrification programme demonstrates how environmental responsibility and operational efficiency can reinforce one another.\n\nThis is a reminder that the most sustainable terminal is often the most efficient one.\n\nRainwater harvesting, condensate water reuse, digital documentation, intelligent energy management systems and smart operational technologies may appear to be separate initiatives. Viewed together, however, they represent a broader approach to resource management.\n\nWaste is no longer viewed simply as rubbish to be disposed of. It includes wasted fuel, wasted electricity, wasted water, wasted paper and wasted time. Reducing each of these improves both environmental performance and operational productivity.\n\nThis is one of the most significant shifts taking place in the global port industry. Sustainability is moving away from isolated environmental projects towards continuous operational improvement.\n\nThe evolution is also changing how ports view their relationship with the communities and natural environments around them.\n\nPorts are often seen primarily as transport infrastructure. In reality, they influence coastlines, urban environments, local economies and marine ecosystems. Their responsibilities therefore extend beyond the terminal gate.\n\nThis broader understanding is reflected in initiatives such as CICT\u2019s long-standing support for Sri Lanka\u2019s Wheelchair Tennis programme, which has helped athletes compete internationally while promoting inclusion through sport, and the Ambassador Cup, in partnership with the University of Colombo, which brings together students from universities across Sri Lanka through badminton, encouraging positive engagement and helping foster a more inclusive and constructive university culture."
  },
