    "md5": "a9ca2a7db089d4669eb5acb97a5cdbf2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1676,
    "chunk_text": "Reporting is only one component of a meaningful sustainability journey. Strong governance is equally essential to ensure that sustainability considerations are embedded in organisational decision-making, oversight and accountability.\n\nBy strengthening the role of finance professionals in sustainability governance, the Code reflects the evolving responsibilities of the profession. Finance professionals are increasingly expected not only to report on an organisation\u2019s performance, but also to help identify emerging risks and opportunities, support responsible decision-making and contribute to long-term value creation.\n\nThe sustainability transition must also extend beyond the corporate sector. Public-sector institutions manage significant resources and play a central role in delivering services, developing infrastructure and advancing national development priorities.\n\nIn recognition of this, the Association of Public Finance Accountants of Sri Lanka (APFASL), the public sector wing of CA Sri Lanka, is in the process of introducing Sustainability Reporting Standard (SRS) 1, issued by the International Public Sector Accounting Standards Board (IPSASB), with a focus on strengthening sustainability reporting in the public sector.\n\nThe introduction of SRS 1 represents an important step towards enhancing transparency and accountability in public-sector sustainability reporting. It will support public-sector entities in communicating information on sustainability-related matters and the impacts of their activities, helping stakeholders better understand how public resources and national development efforts intersect with broader sustainability objectives."
  },
  {
