    "chunk_id": "2026-07-23-dailymirrorlk-f4278eba-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "c29901a43fa1cdf01d376cf5801e6a98",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1996,
    "chunk_text": "Age of AI engineers has arrived: 99x\n\nAs artificial intelligence (AI) reshapes the software industry, 99x is taking a distinctive approach by reimagining product engineering through its AI-Development Lifecycle (AI-DLC) model and Xianix, a platform designed to integrate autonomous AI agents into engineering teams. In this interview, 99x Product Engineering CEO Hasith Yaggahavita shares his perspectives with Mirror Business on the evolving role of engineers, importance of human capabilities in an AI-driven world, Sri Lanka\u2019s growing influence in global product engineering and long-term vision guiding 99x as it navigates one of the most significant technological\u00a0shifts of our time.\n\nQ 99x has spoken about moving from the traditional Software Development Lifecycle to what you now call the AI-Development Lifecycle or AI-DLC. For someone outside the technology industry, what fundamentally changes when AI becomes part of the development process from day one?\n\nThe core shift is that the tasks historically considered core engineering skills, such as code generation and testing against specifications, are now being performed by AI with greater speed and quality. For service-oriented software companies, this represents a critical inflection point that requires a fundamental rethink of how the industry operates.\n\nQ Many companies are adapting to AI by adding new tools and features but 99x has reimagined the entire development process through its AI-DLC model, something only a few companies are pursuing globally. What drove this bold shift?\n\nMost 99x customers are product and platform companies that entrust us with their most critical asset, which is their core software product. In competitive global markets, commodity AI tools alone will not differentiate organisations. Real competitive advantage comes from orchestrating three things together: best-in-class processes, best-in-class people and best-in-class AI tools. That orchestration itself becomes the strategic asset."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-23-dailymirrorlk-f4278eba",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-f4278eba",
    "date_str": "2026-07-23",
    "description": "Age of AI engineers has arrived: 99x",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business/Age-of-AI-engineers-has-arrived-99x/215-346468",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1784830006.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-23-dailymirrorlk-f4278eba-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "a9a8479268ccfa3f110beca951bc83a6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1902,
    "chunk_text": "Most 99x customers are product and platform companies that entrust us with their most critical asset, which is their core software product. In competitive global markets, commodity AI tools alone will not differentiate organisations. Real competitive advantage comes from orchestrating three things together: best-in-class processes, best-in-class people and best-in-class AI tools. That orchestration itself becomes the strategic asset.\n\nHuman value goes beyond knowledge and skills. It centres on attributes such as courage, passion, ambition and drive, qualities that differentiate humans from AI. Engineers must evolve to provide strategic direction, bring user context and empathy and make critical decisions. These capabilities, context, direction and judgement are what professionals need to develop as AI becomes integral to workflows.\n\nQ 99x developed Xianix to support this transformation. Could you explain what Xianix is designed to solve and why platforms like this will become increasingly important in the future of product engineering?\n\nIndividual productivity tools do not translate into team or product-level gains. Xianix solves this by creating central autonomous AI agents that function as team members with specific roles and responsibilities, rather than as copilots on individual machines.\n\nOrganisations can build their own digital workers tailored to their needs, creating intellectual property assets rather than relying on commodity tools. This represents a fundamentally different orchestration model.\n\nQ 99x is taking Xianix to an international audience through its launch in Oslo. What does this moment represent for 99x and for Sri Lanka\u2019s growing influence in global product engineering and AI innovation?\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s strength is not its scale; it is its quality, ingenuity and highly skilled workforce that is willing to challenge conventions and lead innovation."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-07-23-dailymirrorlk-f4278eba",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-f4278eba",
