    "description": "Age of AI engineers has arrived: 99x",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/business/Age-of-AI-engineers-has-arrived-99x/215-346468",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1784830006.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-07-23-dailymirrorlk-f4278eba-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "889e73ba85890a3307bd0231204e9f92",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1986,
    "chunk_text": "Q 99x is taking Xianix to an international audience through its launch in Oslo. What does this moment represent for 99x and for Sri Lanka\u2019s growing influence in global product engineering and AI innovation?\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s strength is not its scale; it is its quality, ingenuity and highly skilled workforce that is willing to challenge conventions and lead innovation.\n\nQ There is growing discussion globally about whether AI will replace jobs. Based on what you are seeing within 99x, how do you believe AI will reshape the role of software engineers rather than diminish it?\n\nI think the discussion around AI replacing engineers misses the bigger reality. Historically, every major technological shift has increased the value of people who are able to adapt, learn and evolve alongside it.\n\nWhat we are seeing at 99x are two clear shifts. First, engineers need to move beyond repetitive execution and develop stronger skills in systems thinking, customer understanding, problem-solving and working alongside AI-driven systems. Second, organisations need to treat continuous reskilling and upskilling as a core business priority rather than an optional learning initiative.\n\nThat is why we are investing heavily in building AI capability internally through hackathons, mentorship and AI-focused engineering training.\n\nQ Sri Lanka continues to produce exceptional technology talent, with 99x also recognised for nurturing world-class engineers. What makes Sri Lankan talent globally competitive and what qualities does 99x look for in the next generation of innovators?\n\nTwo decades of working with some of the most demanding product companies in the world have shaped a certain kind of engineer here, one who is technically rigorous but also accountable, adaptable and curious. That combination is not common.\n\nWhat we look for now goes beyond technical ability. We look for people who are curious about the problem, not just the solution. People who want ownership, not just a task."
  },
