    "md5": "541bc2500af61ac5bda94f2276e9d0fb",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1754,
    "chunk_text": "Two decades of working with some of the most demanding product companies in the world have shaped a certain kind of engineer here, one who is technically rigorous but also accountable, adaptable and curious. That combination is not common.\n\nWhat we look for now goes beyond technical ability. We look for people who are curious about the problem, not just the solution. People who want ownership, not just a task.\n\nWe have themed 2026 as the \u2018Year of Proximity\u2019, emphasising closer relationships with customers, closer collaboration across markets and closer alignment between engineering, business strategy and innovation. I believe that proximity is what creates relevance and relevance is what sustains long-term impact.\n\nQ Leadership in technology today requires balancing innovation with responsibility. How do you ensure that rapid advancements in AI remain ethical, practical and genuinely beneficial for customers and society?\n\nAs AI systems become more autonomous, organisations need strong human accountability built into the process. That is why our approach has always been human-centred. Even within platforms such as Xianix, AI can recommend and accelerate execution but humans remain responsible for critical decisions, product direction and oversight.\n\nBeyond business, there is also a broader social dimension. We invest in universities, mentorship programmes, hackathons and AI learning initiatives because the future of responsible AI depends on how well we prepare the next generation of engineers to work with these technologies thoughtfully and ethically.\n\nQ 99x recently received major industry awards and beyond celebrating achievement, what do these recognitions say about the culture and direction of 99x as an organisation?"
  },
